{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent bearish bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when the market transitions from a dip to a sustained rally, the model follows too slowly and misses the slope. Add a momentum/trend-following term based on the last 5–10 days’ return, with stronger weight at longer horizons.",
    "Rally underestimation after local lows: following a multi-day decline into a trough, realized prices rebound more sharply than forecast. If recent 10-day drawdown exceeds a threshold, increase the next 2–3 weeks forecast path (convex rebound adjustment).",
    "Horizon-dependent error structure: short horizon (1–5d) errors are small and mixed; medium/long horizon (10–30d) errors become increasingly negative. Use a piecewise correction curve by forecast day rather than a single global bias.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too flat versus realized swings during uptrends. Increase forecast variance conditional on positive momentum regimes (wider, higher path), but keep near-term adjustments modest."
  ]
}
